+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:BEGIN BEHAVIOR -->
+### Behavior
+
+Without a navigation polyline, a unique offline road corridor can supply curve speed preview. It ends at forks and requires fresh GPS, heading and near-vision agreement. Navigation geometry has priority; other lower speed limits remain active.
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:END BEHAVIOR -->
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:BEGIN USAGE -->
+### Use
+
+Keep this switch on and select turn-speed mode 2. While parked on unmetered Wi-Fi, all Taiwan map groups download automatically: main island, Penghu, Kinmen and Matsu. Completed regions work offline. Turning this switch off removes the OSM contribution.
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:END USAGE -->
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:BEGIN REFERENCE -->
+### Reference
+
+Road centerlines are not lane-level maps. Missing tiles, parallel-road ambiguity or stale position prevent new OSM constraints. This does not select directions, steer or supply navigation-model guidance. Fresh device GPS is required independently of phone navigation. Only unique one-way motorway corridors explicitly tagged with 3–4 lanes allow bounded centreline offsets, with near-path shape checks and a tighter lane-radius speed cap. `gps=device/lane=...` and mismatch `e...` aid diagnosis. Physical C4 performance still requires validation.
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:END REFERENCE -->
